+The sampled obligation is `abs(gpu - originF64) <= 2e-6 + 2e-5 * abs(originF64)` for every coordinate of a finite reference result, with equal return shapes. The original f64 evaluator receives the exact f32 inputs sent to CUDA. The current revision supplies the kernels, including separate inner and outer accumulation after a split. The design probe found a one-word-step difference between GB10 `expf` and stage-1 f32, and a maximum of 15.6% of this budget across 40,960 sampled values. [Probe record](../scripts/04-probe-result.json)
 
-<!-- Provide background context needed to use this reference -->
+**Framework source changes: none.** A typed transformation relates an artifact to text. Its differential invariant dispatches all missing case indices in one GPU invocation, records the counterexample with coordinates, and replays completed verdicts under the same revision, function, version and source digest. The only tool change isolates identical concurrent calls in private remote working directories while retaining the full-input binary cache. [Checked pass](../../../../../../src/tensor/cuda-contract.ts) · [Remote tool](../../../../../../tools/cuda-remote.mjs)
 
-## Quick Reference
+## Full-size measurements
 
-<!-- Provide copy/paste-ready content, API contracts, or quick-look tables -->
+The original corpus functions ran with seed 22, uniform signed f32 inputs and layernorm epsilon `1e-5`. The SHA-256 recorded per function covers parameter data in declaration order. Each GPU value is the median of three intervals on one stream after one warm-up, under the tool's timing lock. CUDA events enclose only the launch sequence. The interpreter value is the elapsed wall time for one full default-f32 `runTix` on this shared machine after a small-shape warm-up. Input generation, compilation and reporting are outside both intervals. The devices used Node 24.18.0 locally and the NVIDIA GB10 with nvcc 13.0.88, `-O3 -arch=sm_121`, remotely. [Reproduction script](../scripts/05-benchmark.mjs)
 
